A||N||6||JustaV6||Fan not running?||||1||
Z||000000||JustaV6||08-05-2001||10:00 PM||||86 GT<BR>Having problems getting the radiator fan to start running. I replaced the fan switch with a low temp one and also a new relay. It will run when I jumper it so I know the fan runs. Also my temp gauge on the dash doesn't seem to be working. Any troubleshooting tips<BR>would be appreciated. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||08-05-2001||11:17 PM||||do a search under technical. Should be lots of discusion found there.<P>maybe this will help tooo.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010548.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010548.html</A> <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/petesigsm.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||starfighter007@msn.com||08-06-2001||08:36 PM||||I would look at your temp switch you need to check it an the wireing to it .but more then likley It sounds like the switch.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-06-2001||08:53 PM||||See if you have 12V on the main supply lead at the relay. Big red wire. No power means the fuse link quit. The FAN E fuse also has to be good but it's not waht actually powers the fan.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||08-07-2001||12:36 AM||||Will the fan run with A/C turned on?<BR>NO?, check relay voltage and relay.<BR>(along with what Theogre said)<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||08-07-2001||02:15 AM||||When mine had that problem it wouldnt run with the a/c either. Turned out to be a burnt cuircut path on the a/c control head board. Pretty easy to spot when the head is removed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Butter||08-07-2001||12:01 PM||||All the above sounds like the proper advise but if all that fails you might want to check and see if there is a drag on the fan when everything is off and you spin the fan over by hand.  I had a fan motor that some of the magnets had come unglued from the outer housing due to rust apparently.  Since the parts houses around here could not match up my fan I got some sandpaper and JB Weld and glued them suckers (magnets) back in using pencils to keep the magnets apart from one another and relatively evenly spaced till the epoxy had cured. Be sure to keep the polarity and order of the magnets in the proper order in which they come out.  I also relubed the bearings and bushings while I had it apart.  Just what I had encounter when I had a simular problem.||||reg||1
